L000255	\subsection{Model details}\label{app:models}
L000256	
L000257	The relation between $\chieff$ and $\chip$ when secondary spin is small is well-described by
L000258	\begin{align}
L000259	    \chip^2 = a_1^2-(1+q)^2\chieff^2.
L000260	\end{align} This defines the boundary of an ellipse,
L000261	\begin{align}
L000262	    F(\chieff,\chip|\chi_c,q_c)=\left(\frac{1+q_c}{\chi_c}\right)^2\chieff^2+\frac{\chip^2}{\chi_c^2}-1 = 0,
L000263	\end{align} where $\chi_c$ and $q_c$ are the characteristic primary spin and mass ratio. The distribution has some spread around this curve due to variation in mass ratio, primary spin, and secondary spin. To capture this width, we model the probability density for this component as a Gaussian in the distance to the curve. The perpendicular distance from a point $(\chieff,\chip)$ to $F$ is, to linear order
L000264	\begin{align}
L000265	    d(\chieff,\chip|\chi_c,q_c)&\approx\frac{|F(\chieff,\chip|\chi_c,q_c)|}{\Vert\nabla F(\chieff,\chip|\chi_c,q_c)\Vert}\\
L000266	    &= \frac{\left|\frac{\chieff^2}{(\chi_c/(1+q_c))^2}+\frac{\chip^2}{\chi_c^2}-1\right|}{2\left(\frac{\chieff^2}{(\chi_c/(1+q_c))^4}+\frac{\chip^2}{\chi_c^4}\right)^{1/2}}.
L000267	\end{align} The density at $(\chieff,\chip)$ is thus
L000268	\begin{align}
L000269	    \mathcal N(\chieff,\chip|\chi_c, q_c, \sigma) \propto\exp\left(-\frac{d(\chieff,\chip|\chi_c,q_c)^2}{2\sigma^2}\right).
L000270	\end{align} An isotropic tilt angle distribution, as expected for hierarchical mergers, corresponds to a $\sin\tau_1$ distribution  diverging at unity, $p(\sin\tau_1)\propto\sin\tau_1/\sqrt{1-\sin^2\tau_1}$. The marginal distribution of $\chip$ thus peaks at $\chi_c$ following
L000271	\begin{align}
L000272	    p(\chi_p)\propto\begin{cases}
L000273	        \frac{\chip}{\sqrt{\chi_c^2-\chip^2}},& \chi_p<\chi_c;\\
L000274	        0, & \chi_p\ge\chi_c.
L000275	    \end{cases}
L000276	\end{align} As divergent behavior and sharp cutoffs are problematic to model, we instead accommodate this falloff with the filter $f(\chip)=\chip/\sqrt{1-\chip^2}$. While this approximation lacks steepness toward $\chi_c$, we find the Gaussian overlay accommodates the necessary density toward $\chi_c$ such that this model can reproduce the marginal $\chip$ distribution of simulated 1G+2G mergers~\cite{Kremer:2019iul, Rodriguez:2019huv} quite accurately.
L000277	The total probability density for the ellipse component is
L000278	\begin{align}
L000279	    p_\mathrm{1G+2G}(\chieff,\chip|\chi_c,q_c,\sigma) = \mathcal N(\chieff,\chip|\chi_c, q_c, \sigma) f(\chip).
L000280	\end{align}
L000281	The width parameter $\sigma$ captures the expected spread in the $\chieff$--$\chip$ distribution due to the varying mass ratios and precise spin values of 2G mergers. We fit for the width and characteristic spin and mass ratio values with priors listed in Tab.~\ref{tab:priors}.
L000282	
L000283	\subsection{Hierarchical Bayesian inference}\label{app:inference}
L000284	
L000285	We sample the population posterior using \texttt{Dynesty}~\cite{Speagle:2019ivv} and \texttt{Bilby}~\cite{bilby_paper} with the population likelihood~\cite{Mandel:2018mve, Vitale:2020aaz} as implemented in \texttt{GWPopulation}~\cite{Talbot:2024yqw}. Our catalog consists of the 153 binary black holes in GWTC-4~\cite{gwtc4cat} and the special events GW241011 and GW241110~\cite{gw1011disc}. We use the \textsc{Mixed} waveform results in the publicly-released posterior samples~\cite{gwtc4release, gw1011release} for most events, but reduce the parameter estimation contribution to the log-likelihood variance by instead using the \textsc{IMRPhenomXPHM}~\cite{Pratten:2020ceb} results for GW150914 and GW200129, which have fewer than 5000 \textsc{Mixed} samples. We estimate survey sensitivity with the public set of BBH injections recovered by search pipelines spanning all four observing runs~\cite{Essick:2025zed}.
L000286	
L000287	We limit the log-likelihood variance from the Monte Carlo approximations in the likelihood to be smaller than 1, a condition shown to be sufficient to prevent bias in the posterior~\cite{Essick:2022ojx, Heinzel:2025ogf}. We compute the error statistic introduced in Ref.~\cite{Heinzel:2025ogf} and find an information loss of 0.18 bits, within the suggested threshold. To test the impact of the variance cut, we also run with a limit of 4. The primary difference is in the inferred 1G+1G $\chip$ distribution, which is narrower and closer to zero than found with a variance cut of 1. While the data seemingly prefer small $\chip$ values, our inferential capability is limited in that regime predominantly due to selection variance.
